We're using Express with a bunch of middleware, and I'm seeing this pattern a lot:

```js
app.use('/api/v1/users', require('./routes/users'));
app.use('/api/v1/orgs', require('./routes/orgs'));
app.use('/api/v1/projects', require('./routes/projects'));
```

in server.js. It works, but feels brittle. If we add 20 more routes, this file will be unreadable. Should we switch to dynamic route loading from a directory, like `fs.readdirSync('./routes')` and auto-mount? Or stick with explicit imports but group them into a single routes/index.js? Or go full MVC with a router registry?

Consider boot time safety, editor support, debugging clarity, and risk of silent failures (e.g. typo in filename). This isn’t about perf — it’s about maintainability at scale. Make a call.

